---
import BrandMark from '../components/BrandMark.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import Snippet from '../components/Snippet.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '../lib/config';

const installExample = `vp add hono @hono/node-server
vp add -D shinro`;

const configExample = `import { shinro } from 'shinro';
import { defineConfig } from 'vite-plus';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [shinro()],
});`;

const tsconfigExample = `{
  "extends": "shinro/tsconfig"
}`;

const mountExample = `import { logger } from 'hono/logger';
import { defineApp } from 'shinro/app';
import { routes } from 'shinro/routes';

const app = defineApp()
  .use('*', logger())
  .route('/', routes());

export default app;`;

const routeExample = `import { defineHandler } from 'shinro/app';

export const GET = defineHandler((c) => {
  return c.json({ id: c.req.param('id') }, 200);
});`;

const clientExample = `import { defineClient } from '@acme/api/client';

const client = defineClient('http://localhost:3000');
const response = await client.api.users[':id'].$get({
  param: { id: 'usr_123' },
});

if (response.status === 200) {
  const user = await response.json();
}`;

const routeDir = 'src/routes/';

const routes = [
  { file: 'index.ts', result: '/' },
  { file: 'health.ts', result: '/health' },
  { file: 'api/users.ts', result: '/api/users' },
  { file: 'api/users/$id.ts', result: '/api/users/:id' },
  { file: '(authed)/orders.ts', result: '/orders' },
  { file: 'files/$...path.ts', result: '/files/:path{.+}' },
  { file: '[index].ts', result: '/index' },
  { file: '_middleware.ts', result: 'Applies to all routes' },
];

const docs = `${siteConfig.repository}/blob/main/docs`;
---

<Layout>
  <header class="mb-5">
    <BrandMark class="size-5 text-black" />
    <h1 class="mt-3 mb-0.5">Shinro</h1>
    <p class="text-zinc-500">File-based routing for Hono</p>
  </header>

  <div class="mb-8">
    <p>
      Shinro turns your files into routes for <a
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        href="https://hono.dev"
        class="link">Hono ↗</a
      >. Add one <a
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        href="https://viteplus.dev"
        class="link">Vite ↗</a
      > plugin and Shinro will find your route files, build a router you can mount
      anywhere, and generate a fully typed client for your API.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <SectionHeading step={1} title="Install" />

    <p>
      Install Shinro alongside Hono. If you are using Node.js, you will also need
      Hono's Node adapter. Bun users can leave it out and use
      <code>Bun.serve()</code> instead.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-3">
      <Snippet code={installExample} lang="sh" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <SectionHeading step={2} title="Add the plugin" />

    <p>
      Add <code>shinro()</code> to your Vite config. That is the whole
      integration: the plugin handles route discovery, development, and RPC type
      generation on both Node.js and Bun.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-3">
      <Snippet code={configExample} title="vite.config.ts" />
    </div>

    <p class="mt-3">
      Then extend Shinro's TypeScript config so imports such as <code
        >shinro/app</code
      > and <code>shinro/routes</code> work without a custom
      <code>paths</code> setup.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-3">
      <Snippet code={tsconfigExample} lang="json" title="tsconfig.json" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <SectionHeading step={3} title="Mount the router" />

    <p>
      <code>routes()</code> gives you a regular Hono router. Mount it
      wherever it fits in your middleware chain. Hono carries the route schema into
      <code>typeof app</code>, so your app and RPC client share the same contract.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-3">
      <Snippet code={mountExample} title="src/app.ts" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <SectionHeading step={4} title="Routes" />

    <p>
      Folders and filenames become URL paths. Start a segment with
      <code>$</code> for a parameter or <code>$...</code> for a catch-all.
      Use a <code>(group)</code> folder to share middleware without changing the
      URL. Add <code>_middleware.ts</code> to wrap every route in a directory,
      and use <code>[brackets]</code> when you need a literal path segment.
    </p>

    <div class="table-block mt-3">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>File</th>
            <th>Result</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            routes.map((route) => (
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <span class="text-zinc-400">{routeDir}</span>{route.file}
                </td>
                <td class="text-zinc-600">{route.result}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-3">
      Each route file exports a handler named after the HTTP method it supports.
      Exporting the handler is all you need to register the route.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-3">
      <Snippet code={routeExample} title="src/routes/api/users/$id.ts" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <SectionHeading step={5} title="Typed Client" />

    <p>
      Shinro generates a Hono client from your app, with types for every route,
      parameter, and response. Export it from your API package, then use it
      wherever you call the API.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-3">
      <Snippet code={clientExample} />
    </div>

    <p class="mt-3">
      Response types are tied to their status. Check for <code>200</code> and
      TypeScript knows the exact shape of <code>user</code>. Early middleware
      responses, such as <code>401</code>, are included too.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="mt-8 space-y-3">
    <p class="text-zinc-600">
      See the <a
        href={`${docs}/file-route-conventions.md`}
        class="link text-black">file route conventions</a
      > for the full set of filename rules, or read the <a
        href={`${docs}/SPEC.md`}
        class="link text-black">v0.1 spec</a
      > for the complete contract. You can also explore the <a
        href={`${siteConfig.repository}/tree/main/examples/api`}
        class="link text-black">example API</a
      > to see everything working in one runnable project.
    </p>

    <p class="text-zinc-600">
      Shinro runs on Node.js and Bun. It is MIT licensed and open source on <a
        href={siteConfig.repository}
        class="link text-black">GitHub</a
      >. Built by <a
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        href="https://x.com/imarikchakma"
        class="link text-black">Arik Chakma</a
      >.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
